From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 20:34:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Winios: give the foreground back after a focus loss

A focus loss deactivates the foreground window on its own thread, which
leaves Wine with no foreground window. Focus back called
NtUserSetForegroundWindow(saved) from whichever guest thread drained the
event; from a thread of another Wine process the server refuses it (the
process is not the foreground's and its input is older), so the
foreground stayed empty: every key and character went nowhere and every
touch was dropped for want of a client area (WoW after the iOS Passwords
sheet: focus back fg=0x0 -> 0x0 ok=0, then 173 dropped touches).

Focus back now uses Wine's internal foreground request, which any thread
may make (what winemac.drv uses for the host's activations), on the
saved window if it can still be activated, else the topmost activatable
window of its process, else the topmost of any (the host app's
host_focus_pick). Input posted after it waits at the ring's head until
a window is the foreground again, 0.5 s at most. Input while the app is
in front and no window is the foreground makes the same restore, so a
tap on the game recovers too; after one that did not land, input does
not wait again for 2 s. The window's focused child at the loss gets the
focus back once the window is active again on its own thread.

Linear: PLA-516
Class: host-app
Evidence: app/tests/focus_test.c (host checks; phone gate not run); the owner's WoW log of 2026-11-01 (focus back ok=0, fg 0 afterwards)
Offered-upstream: no; the host app's focus events
